  "summary": "After terminating its Flock Safety contract in April, Oshkosh Police Department is forming a cybersecurity-led advisory committee to vet a replacement ALPR vendor and establish security and operational best practices. The article recaps prior trust and data-access concerns that led to the cancellation and notes similar terminations by other Wisconsin municipalities.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Oshkosh Police Department hasn't begun looking for a successor to Flock Safety yet, saying it intends to set up an advisory committee to identify a future automated license plate reader vendor.",
      "paragraph_idx": 4
    },
    {
      "quote": "Common Council unanimously voted not to renew its contract for the contentious cameras in April less than 24 hours after approving an amended one-year agreement with Flock.",
      "paragraph_idx": 9
    },
    {
      "quote": "OPD powered off each of Oshkosh's 26 Flock cameras and covered the license plate readers with a bag to ensure they cannot collect data.",
      "paragraph_idx": 11
    }
